dying stars can go out  with a bang an enormous explosion called  a supernova  these cosmic pyrotechnics shoot chemical  elements and energetic particles out  into space  and the explosion can release more  energy than the sun will  over its entire life but scientists  don't fully understand the  extreme physics of supernova explosions  that's because there's nothing like them  on earth or  is there  [Music]  supernovas are fascinating they are not  just distant explosions they are the  source of  elements like the iron in our blood and  the calcium in our bones  but they're really far away so there's  no hopping on a spacecraft to check one  out  in our galaxy supernovas only happen a  few times a century  so spotting one nearby with the  telescope is a waiting game  so scientists are using laboratory  explosions to bring the physics of a  supernova  down to earth here's what we do know  when a really big star gets old it  eventually runs out of fuel  its core collapses and rebounds that  triggers a wave of high density and  pressure that plows outward through the  star  when this wave reaches the surface the  star explodes  igniting a light show that could  outshine its entire home  galaxy a mix of electrically charged  particles or plasma  blasts into space at about 10 000  kilometers per second  the explosion leaves behind a remnant an  expanding bubble of gas and plasma  within the remnant itself a shock wave  forms and  flings out charged particles to energies  that outdo  even the biggest particle accelerators  on earth  and that remnant it can stick around for  hundreds of thousands of years  it's impossible to fully replicate the  physics of a supernova in the laboratory  for one thing a real supernova would  blow up everything in sight  but the same physics governs how charged  particles flow  in giant supernovas and in lab made  blasts that are shrunk down to a  manageable size  scientists also focus on one bit of the  physics at a time  they might try to reproduce the  supernova remnants shockwave for example  but not all the other complicated  physics that goes on during the  explosion  if you want to understand supernovas you  might as well start bic  the national ignition facility at  lawrence livermore national laboratory  in california  has 192 laser beams that can momentarily  unload more power than the entire united  states uses  in a given instant one team of  physicists  is using these lasers to study shock  waves in supernova remnants  this kind of shock wave shows up when  the blasts plasma  collides with other plasma that's  already hanging out in interstellar  space  there electric and magnetic fields grow  stronger  these electromagnetic fields not only  help form the shock wave  but they also ping-pong charge particles  back and forth across it  each time the particles cross they speed  up eventually reaching extreme energies  but the details of how the magnetic  fields form and  how the particles are accelerated have  been a mystery  scientists use lasers like those at the  national ignition facility to produce  colliding bursts of plasma  revealing how magnetic fields form  within  in a recent experiment scientists also  reproduce a supernova remnant  shock wave showing for the first time  how particles are accelerated  if you don't happen to have 192 laser  beams at your disposal  good old-fashioned explosives can also  do the trick  researchers at georgia tech are focused  on recreating the early moments of a  supernova  let's rewind before stars go supernova  they are layered like an onion  with heavier elements like iron in the  center and lighter elements like  hydrogen on the outside  when the pressure wave inside the star  rams through the onion layers  elements from the stars innards are  ejected  to understand how those layers behave in  the early moments of a supernova  the georgia tech team came up with a  dynamite strategy  build a pizza slice-shaped blast chamber  to mimic a wedge of a star  fill it with two layers of gas to mimic  the onion structure  and add a powerful explosive to capture  the blast's aftermath the researchers  illuminate the gas with laser light  and they let her rip as the resulting  blast wave plows through  the gas layers form bubbles spikes and  mushroom shaped tendrils  and some material flies outward the  experiments show that these kinds of  explosions  can mimic a supernova and they might  help researchers better understand  how elements are ejected from within a  star and  better understanding these early stages  of a supernova  could help researchers explain the  complex structures that are seen later  in supernova remnants  supernovas are some of the most extreme  environments in the cosmos by studying  them we are exploring uncharted realms  of physics bringing supernovas down to  earth with experiments could  help us understand other cosmic  mysteries like how magnetic fields form  in  other realms of the universe  while the bang might spell the end of a  star it's just the beginning  the elements it releases go out to  enrich the cosmos  the shock wave formed in the supernova  remnant could collapse  clouds of gas helping to form new stars  and energetic particles accelerated by  that shock wave  will streak across the sky perhaps one  day  crash landing on earth a supernova is  the culmination of a star's life  but it's the start of countless other  stories  you
 

